<Ui>
    <!--
        The reference's SHARED widget kit, in our own terms. Interface\FrameXML\UIPanelTemplates.xml
        is the SPECIFICATION — the template names, the child names, the art, every offset and every
        handler's observable effect are read off the extracted 1.12.1 file, and the line numbers
        cited below are that file's. Nothing here is required to be *spelled* the way it spells it,
        and the two Lua handlers are not.

        WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Every window benilla has built so far declared its own private
        copy of these — `BenillaQuestPanelButtonTemplate`, `BenillaTrainerPanelButtonTemplate`,
        `BenillaMacroPanelButtonTemplate` and a dozen siblings, each the same four state textures on
        the same UI-Panel-Button art. That was fine while the only consumer was our own XML. It
        stopped being fine the day `CreateFrame`'s fourth argument started working (decision 1203):
        an addon writes `CreateFrame("Button", "MyBtn", UIParent, "UIPanelButtonTemplate")` and
        nothing else, and 185 of the corpus's 218 addons' template call sites name templates we had
        never declared. These are those templates, under the reference's own names — the 1190 rule
        (a name the shipped 1.12 UI defines is FrameXML's, and belongs in `assets/ui`) applied to
        the ranked list `addon_harness` now prints.

        Every name here is in `reference/1.12-globals.tsv`; none is invented.

        NO DEVIATIONS — the state textures below are the reference's, `<NormalTexture
        inherits="UIPanelButtonUpTexture"/>` and all. That is worth a note only because it was not
        true when this file first landed: the loader expanded `inherits=` on `<Layers>` regions and
        on nested `<Frames>` children but not on a Button's state-texture or a Slider's thumb, so a
        faithful transcription produced buttons with no art AND NO ERROR. The first version carried
        the resolved art inline as a workaround; both gaps are now fixed in
        `loader/widgets.rs` (`apply_button`/`apply_slider` call `expand_region`, the same call a
        `<Layers>` region gets) and the workaround is gone. Reverting either fix makes
        `a_panel_button_from_the_template_labels_and_paints` and
        `a_scroll_frame_from_the_template_wires_its_bar_two_parents_deep` fail.

        NOT CARRIED, deliberately:
        · `FauxScrollFrameTemplate` (ref l.213-225) — benilla already ships one under the reference's
          own name in ScrollTemplates.xml, built as a plain `<Frame>` over `BenillaScrollBarTemplate`
          (decisions 0247/0250/0251). Two declarations of one name would race; re-pointing ours at
          the reference's `<ScrollFrame>` shape is its own job with its own risk, not this pass's.
        · `TabButtonTemplate` (ref l.303-402) — already declared in UiPanels.xml, with its
          fit/highlight deviations recorded there (decision 1004).
        (`GameMenuButtonTemplate`, ref l.403-410, was on this list as "already in GameMenuFrame.xml"
        until the colour picker needed it from above that file's LAST-in-the-toc seat. It is below
        now, in the reference's own file for it, and GameMenuFrame.xml's private copy is gone.)

        AND WHAT THIS SUPERSEDES BUT DOES NOT DELETE: `UIPanelButtonTemplate` below is the shared
        original of `BenillaQuestPanelButtonTemplate`,
        `BenillaCraftPanelButtonTemplate`, `BenillaQuestLogPanelButtonTemplate`,
        `BenillaFriendsPanelButtonTemplate`, `BenillaTrainerPanelButtonTemplate`,
        `BenillaMacroPanelButtonTemplate`, `BenillaTradeSkillPanelButtonTemplate` and
        `BenillaDressUpPanelButtonTemplate` — eight copies of the same eight lines. Re-pointing those
        windows at this template is a separate, mechanical pass; nothing here changes them.
    -->

    <Script><![CDATA[
        -- The two scroll-frame handlers `UIPanelScrollFrameTemplate` below calls by name. Their
        -- contract — the names, the arguments, and every branch's observable effect — is the
        -- reference's (UIPanelTemplates.lua l.150-157 and l.254-305); the code is ours.
        -- `ScrollFrame_OnLoad` and the whole `FauxScrollFrame_*` family already live in
        -- ScrollTemplates.xml, which loads immediately before this file; these two are the
        -- remainder of that kit.

        -- One wheel notch pans by half a bar-height, up or down. The wheel is SOUNDLESS, unlike the
        -- arrow buttons — the reference's own split, and the director's (trainer_tests.rs).
        function ScrollFrameTemplate_OnMouseWheel(value)
            local scrollBar = getglobal(this:GetName() .. "ScrollBar")
            local step = scrollBar:GetHeight() / 2
            if value > 0 then
                step = -step
            end
            scrollBar:SetValue(scrollBar:GetValue() + step)
        end

        -- Reconcile a ScrollFrame's bar, arrows and thumb to a range the engine has just
        -- recomputed. `scrollrange` is the engine's own second argument when this runs as the
        -- handler; a caller who omits it gets the frame's current range instead.
        --
        -- All four children hang off the OWNER's name two `$parent` levels down
        -- (`MyScrollScrollBarScrollUpButton`), so they are resolved ONCE here — the reference walks
        -- back to getglobal at each of the ten points it touches one. `$parentThumbTexture` is what
        -- publishes the last of them; that publication did not exist when this file was first
        -- written (`apply_slider` applied a named thumb's layout and never set the global, the one
        -- asymmetry with a Button's state textures), and the gap was fixed rather than routed
        -- around.
        function ScrollFrame_OnScrollRangeChanged(scrollrange)
            local frame = this
            local scrollbar = getglobal(frame:GetName() .. "ScrollBar")
            local up = getglobal(scrollbar:GetName() .. "ScrollUpButton")
            local down = getglobal(scrollbar:GetName() .. "ScrollDownButton")
            local thumb = getglobal(scrollbar:GetName() .. "ThumbTexture")

            scrollrange = scrollrange or frame:GetVerticalScrollRange()
            local value = min(scrollbar:GetValue(), scrollrange)
            scrollbar:SetMinMaxValues(0, scrollrange)
            scrollbar:SetValue(value)

            -- Nothing to scroll, and two ways of saying so — which one is the OWNER's call:
            -- a hideable bar leaves altogether, a fixed one stays in place and greys out. On the
            -- way back only the DOWN arrow wakes, because the offset is still zero and there is
            -- nowhere up to go; the frame's own vertical-scroll handler below wakes the up arrow
            -- the moment that stops being true.
            local empty = floor(scrollrange) == 0
            if not empty then
                down:Show()
                up:Show()
                scrollbar:Show()
                down:Enable()
            elseif frame.scrollBarHideable then
                scrollbar:Hide()
                down:Hide()
                up:Hide()
            else
                down:Disable()
                up:Disable()
                down:Show()
                up:Show()
            end
            if empty then thumb:Hide() else thumb:Show() end

            -- The frame's own border (ref l.285-304), if it declares one: all three regions are the
            -- OWNER's to name, so all three are optional, and the pair is decided together because
            -- a half-declared border is no border. Note this leg asks the FRAME for its range
            -- rather than trusting the argument, and wants an exact zero where the bar above floors
            -- first — two different questions, and the reference keeps them apart.
            if frame.scrollBarHideable then
                local bordered = frame:GetVerticalScrollRange() ~= 0
                local top = getglobal(frame:GetName() .. "Top")
                local bottom = getglobal(frame:GetName() .. "Bottom")
                local middle = getglobal(frame:GetName() .. "Middle")
                if top and bottom then
                    if bordered then top:Show() bottom:Show() else top:Hide() bottom:Hide() end
                end
                if middle then
                    if bordered then middle:Show() else middle:Hide() end
                end
            end
        end
    ]]></Script>

